---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import NetworkingWizard from '../components/NetworkingWizard';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
---

<PageLayout
  title="Remote access"
  description="Four ways to reach your homelab from outside the house. Answer a few questions to find yours, then follow its steps."
>
  <NetworkingWizard client:load />

  <h2 id="option-a-duckdns">Option A: DuckDNS and Nginx Proxy Manager</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Best for:</strong> people without a domain who want a free public address such as
    <code>yourname.duckdns.org</code> with automatic Let's Encrypt certificates. Your router must forward ports
    80 and 443 to the server.
  </p>

  <h3>1. Register and copy your token</h3>
  <p>
    Go to <a href="https://www.duckdns.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">duckdns.org</a>, sign in, copy the
    token shown at the top, and create a free subdomain (for example <code>myhomelab-demo</code>).
  </p>

  <h3>2. Add the secrets to <code>stacks/.env</code></h3>
  <CodeBlock title="DuckDNS settings" language="env" code={`DUCKDNS_SUBDOMAIN=myhomelab-demo\nDUCKDNS_TOKEN=xxxxxxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxxxxxxxxxx`} />

  <h3>3. Start the stack</h3>
  <CodeBlock title="Start DuckDNS and Nginx Proxy Manager" code={`cd stacks/networking/option-a-duckdns\ndocker compose up -d`} />

  <h3>4. Add a proxy host</h3>
  <p>
    Open <code>http://YOUR_SERVER_IP:81</code> and sign in with the default account (<code>admin@example.com</code>,
    password <code>changeme</code>).
  </p>
  <div class="callout-warn not-prose">
    Change this email and password as soon as you sign in, before you forward any ports. Anyone who reaches this
    page while the defaults still work controls your proxy.
  </div>
  <p>Then go to <strong>Proxy Hosts</strong>, choose <strong>Add Proxy Host</strong>, and fill in:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Domain Names:</strong> <code>myhomelab-demo.duckdns.org</code></li>
    <li><strong>Forward Hostname / IP:</strong> your server's local address</li>
    <li><strong>Forward Port:</strong> <code>8096</code> (Jellyfin)</li>
    <li>On the <strong>SSL</strong> tab, choose <em>Request a new SSL Certificate</em>, tick <em>Force SSL</em>, and save.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="option-b-cloudflare">Option B: Cloudflare DNS challenge</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Best for:</strong> people with their own domain who want a wildcard certificate without opening port 80.
    Cloudflare can also proxy traffic and absorb attacks.
  </p>

  <h3>1. Create a Cloudflare API token</h3>
  <p>
    In the Cloudflare dashboard go to <strong>My Profile</strong>, then <strong>API Tokens</strong>, then
    <strong>Create Token</strong>, and use the <em>Edit zone DNS</em> template for your domain. Copy the token right
    away; Cloudflare shows it once.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Add it to <code>stacks/.env</code></h3>
  <CodeBlock title="Cloudflare token" language="env" code={`CF_API_TOKEN=your_cloudflare_dns_edit_token_here`} />

  <h3>3. Start the stack</h3>
  <CodeBlock title="Start Nginx Proxy Manager for Cloudflare" code={`cd stacks/networking/option-b-cloudflare\ndocker compose up -d`} />

  <h3>4. Request a wildcard certificate</h3>
  <p>
    In Nginx Proxy Manager (<code>http://YOUR_SERVER_IP:81</code>) open <strong>SSL Certificates</strong>, then
    <strong>Add SSL Certificate</strong>, then <strong>Let's Encrypt</strong>:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Domain Names:</strong> <code>*.yourdomain.com, yourdomain.com</code></li>
    <li>Tick <strong>Use a DNS Challenge</strong> and choose <strong>Cloudflare</strong>.</li>
    <li>Paste <code>dns_cloudflare_api_token = your_cloudflare_dns_edit_token_here</code> into the credentials box and save.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="option-c-custom-domain">Option C: buying a domain</h2>
  <p>
    Your own domain gives your apps addresses such as <code>media.yourdomain.com</code> and
    <code>cloud.yourdomain.com</code>. A <code>.com</code> costs about $10 a year. Three registrars that people
    in the homelab community often recommend:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="https://porkbun.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Porkbun</a>: low renewal prices and free WHOIS privacy.</li>
    <li><a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/products/registrar/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cloudflare Registrar</a>: sells at cost and is already wired into Cloudflare DNS.</li>
    <li><a href="https://www.namecheap.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Namecheap</a>: long-standing registrar with free domain privacy.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Prices change often, so compare renewal prices, not first-year offers.</p>

  <h2 id="option-d-tailscale">Option D: Tailscale</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Best for:</strong> reaching your server from your own phone and laptop only, with no open ports and
    nothing visible on the public internet.
  </p>

  <h3>1. Generate an auth key</h3>
  <p>
    In the <a href="https://login.tailscale.com/admin/machines" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Tailscale admin console</a>,
    go to <strong>Settings</strong>, then <strong>Keys</strong>, then <strong>Generate auth key</strong>. Tick
    <strong>Reusable</strong> and leave <strong>Ephemeral</strong> unticked.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Add it to <code>stacks/.env</code></h3>
  <CodeBlock title="Tailscale auth key" language="env" code={`TAILSCALE_AUTH_KEY=tskey-auth-xxxxxxxxxxxx-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx`} />

  <h3>3. Start the Tailscale container</h3>
  <CodeBlock title="Start Tailscale" code={`cd stacks/networking/option-d-tailscale\ndocker compose up -d`} />

  <h3>4. Check the connection</h3>
  <p>
    Your Tailscale dashboard now lists a machine called <code>homelab</code> with a private Tailscale address. On a
    phone or laptop signed in to the same Tailscale account, open <code>http://homelab:8096</code>.
  </p>
</PageLayout>
